Here are a few ways to write a JavaScript function to return the $n$-th Fibonacci number. I'll provide an iterative solution (efficient for moderate $n$) and a recursive solution (elegant but inefficient for large $n$).

### 1. Iterative Solution (Recommended for performance)

This method uses a loop and is generally the most efficient way to calculate Fibonacci numbers for reasonable inputs, as it avoids the exponential time complexity of naive recursion.

```javascript
/**
 * Calculates the n-th Fibonacci number iteratively.
 * The sequence starts with F(0) = 0 and F(1) = 1.
 *
 * @param {number} n The index of the Fibonacci number to calculate (must be a non-negative integer).
 * @returns {number} The n-th Fibonacci number.
 * @throws {Error} If n is negative.
 */
function fibonacciIterative(n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        throw new Error("Input must be a non-negative integer.");
    }
    if (n === 0) {
        return 0;
    }
    if (n === 1) {
        return 1;
    }

    let a = 0;
    let b = 1;
    let result = 0;

    // Start loop from i=2 up to n
    for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        result = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = result;
    }

    return result;
}

// Example Usage:
console.log(`Fibonacci(0): ${fibonacciIterative(0)}`);   // Output: 0
console.log(`Fibonacci(1): ${fibonacciIterative(1)}`);   // Output: 1
console.log(`Fibonacci(2): ${fibonacciIterative(2)}`);   // Output: 1
console.log(`Fibonacci(7): ${fibonacciIterative(7)}`);   // Output: 13
console.log(`Fibonacci(10): ${fibonacciIterative(10)}`); // Output: 55
```

### 2. Recursive Solution (Simple but Inefficient)

This solution directly follows the mathematical definition ($F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2)$), but it recalculates the same values many times, leading to exponential time complexity ($O(2^n)$).

```javascript
/**
 * Calculates the n-th Fibonacci number recursively.
 * WARNING: This is very inefficient for large values of n.
 *
 * @param {number} n The index of the Fibonacci number to calculate (must be a non-negative integer).
 * @returns {number} The n-th Fibonacci number.
 * @throws {Error} If n is negative.
 */
function fibonacciRecursive(n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        throw new Error("Input must be a non-negative integer.");
