### 2. Recursive Approach (For conceptual understanding)

This approach directly translates the mathematical definition ($F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2)$). **Be cautious:** This is very inefficient for large values of $n$ due to repeated calculations (exponential time complexity, $O(2^n)$).

```typescript
/**
 * Calculates the n-th Fibonacci number recursively.
 * WARNING: This method is highly inefficient for large values of n.
 *
 * @param n The index of the Fibonacci number to calculate (must be a non-negative integer).
 * @returns The n-th Fibonacci number.
 * @throws {Error} If n is negative.
 */
function fibonacciRecursive(n: number): number {
    if (n < 0) {
        throw new Error("Input must be a non-negative integer.");
    }
    if (n === 0) {
        return 0;
    }
    if (n === 1) {
        return 1;
    }
    return fibonacciRecursive(n - 1) + fibonacciRecursive(n - 2);

Here is a TypeScript function that removes duplicates from an array. The most idiomatic and concise way to do this in modern JavaScript/TypeScript is by using a `Set`.

```typescript
/**
 * Removes duplicate elements from an array, returning a new array with only unique values.
 *
 * @param arr The input array which may contain duplicate elements.
 * @returns A new array containing only the unique elements from the input array.
 */
function removeDuplicates<T>(arr: T[]): T[] {
  // A Set only stores unique values. We create a Set from the array,
  // which automatically filters out duplicates. Then, we convert the Set back
  // into an array using the spread operator (...).
  return [...new Set(arr)];
}

// --- Examples ---

// Example 1: Array of numbers
const numbersWithDuplicates: number[] = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 1];
const uniqueNumbers: number[] = removeDuplicates(numbersWithDuplicates);
